ou know i'm in a recording studio and  now we got a hit so you just trip and  fall into wonderful things it's kind of  what you do sometimes the things are  wonderful and sometimes uh it's you know  feces from another species but you know  what you take the good with the bad  micro host of dirty jobs which returns  to the discovery channel january 2nd  also streaming new and original episodes  on discovery plus thank you so much  always a pleasure thanks for it's nice  to see you in person yes you're in the  studio what an honor  [Music]  this is joe koncha with your fox news  commentary coming  up with three weeks left in the year  annual murder records are already broken  in at least a dozen big cities  philadelphia has had more than 500  murders the most there since 1990.  portland oregon's had the most since  1987 austin texas the most since 1984.  there's also albuquerque baton rouge  columbus indianapolis louisville st paul  tucson toledo and rochester new york  other cities had records broken the year  before and gun violence in general has  been higher nationwide you know it is a  convergence of a number of factors in my  view  even we go before coving bill daley is a  former fbi investigator and  international security consultant kind  of pre-coveted we had kind of momentum  by kind of liberal and state legislative  groups and branches uh to reduce  classifications of crimes and i say this  because sometimes when you reduce lower  end crimes it gives rise to higher end  crimes that's the old kind of broken  window theory you know if you let a  broken window remain broken you know  people get in they break into whatever  the facility is they commit other crimes  much like even going back to  uh with some of this even receiving stop  and frisk were in major cities they  would stop people from going uh using  turnstiles to jump over them and they  would arrest those people sometimes  catch them with guns and they would stop  other crimes from happening  but we've seen here is this kind of  erosion both by the classification of  crimes as well as then also the ability  for law enforcement to really and  interdict because in many cases their  hands have been tied uh so you look at  things such as as guns being perhaps  more readily available on the streets or  gun crimes we've seen some really um  atrocious and really impactful images of  shootings caught on video of of people  being shot in the street uh so guns are  out there being used more and more and i  think also combined with what happened  during cobra and some of the perhaps  kind of the self-instilled constraints  on police uh because of concerns over  the allegations of of abuse or or being  in any way kind of racially  discriminatory there have been kind of a  perhaps a recoiling which has resulted  in this gap being filled by these  criminals and unfortunately as i said it  starts from the lower end crimes and  we've seen those certainly over the past  year that lead up into the more horrific  you know homicides and other murders  that take place um do you know is are  there more guns on the streets or fewer  guns being taken off the streets if you  get my meaning  um i'd probably say  it's probably worthy the latter where  there's there's less being taken off the  streets but those are being backfilled  also uh by more coming into the market  so if you're not taking them off the  inventory of those guns being in the  in the population those those let's say  illegal weapons in the population uh are  going up uh because people are not being  stopped they're not you know being  there's no major interdiction going on  aside from maybe some uh some federal  interdiction from  uh atf and some other authorities but on  a local level uh they're finding their  ways in and around major cities uh  finding ways in the hands of of miners  and i would say you know it's because  they're not being taken off the street  uh it's allowed the inventory of those  weapons to propagate uh and with new  weapons coming into the mark into the  street and onto the black market it's  allowed people who in the past may not  have had access to them now have access  you mentioned stop and frisk um there's  another also kind of growing movement  about bail reform we don't necessarily  want innocent people being harassed you  know the crime of walking while black is  a term um we don't necessarily want  innocent people who can't afford to get  out on bail rotting  in county for  six months or whatever just because they  can't afford it it's a really tricky  balance  yeah and the thing about bail reform so  you know i think it's important for us  you know we're having this discussion to  kind of talk about how this all comes  about because you know as much as we do  focus on perhaps more liberal district  attorneys uh state attorney generals  these in many cases are driven uh the  bail reform act and and even sentencing  guidelines are driven by again the  legislative bodies they're the ones who  make the laws they're the ones who make  the laws in in our country whether uh in  the federal or the state level and those  are the ones also providing kind of the  guidelines for for bail i was just  speaking with a uh a judge here in in  new york uh not too long ago about this  and as much as they would like to you  know put a higher bail  on certain violations they're limited  by what has been passed down by by the  state governments by the legislative  branch and so i think i think what's  happening here is we're seeing people  who are out there they know  they know the law they know if they're  they steal under a certain amount of  money they're not going to be prosecuted  uh but again that just i think kind of  encourages other people to say hey  here's a gap that needs to be filled and  they fill it with with criminal acts  whether it be on the lower end or as i  said before many of those then kind of  lead up to  other types of crimes where either gangs  are shooting each other because of turf  issues or attacking citizens themselves  let's talk about those smash and grab  crimes  they're high profile the surveillance  videos is um  provocative for lack of a better word um  what's happening here is it coordinated  is it organized gangs is it  one group or a small group saying  seeing it on tv and hearing that it's  successful and saying geez we could do  that too that's some easy money  uh and you've worked with businesses on  theft prevention a lot what's happening  here right  you know  kind of we all go back uh just a few  years ago kind of at the rise of  social media  um and where people would have these  flash mobs would come out and start to  dance and do other things but then all  of a sudden it took a a very sad and  tragic turn where they kind of these  mobs would show up you know to go and  pilfer things and stake things from  stores this is just a minute of the  manifestation of it i think if we kind  of claw behind the scenes we're going to  see that these are are coordinated uh  they're coordinated on a lower kind of  grassroots level people getting together  saying hey if we all go up and show in  moss into some you know uh high-end  retail uh and we go out and we each take  one one handbag or two handbags uh  depending upon wherever the jurisdiction  is and the uh  kind of that that cut off or being  prosecuted uh we can do it and so it is  this kind of coordinated um wouldn't say  is coordinated from what we may have  seen in the in the kind of older days of  organized crime per se when we had mafia  other uh being out there doing these  things but this is another another  manifestation of it so it is coordinated  and it is something that  you know retailers are really faced with  they're faced with the issue of of how  to protect their their property their  inventory their premises and their  employees um while at the same time  trying to be open for those other  customers who want to come into